\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The square and fixed sites of the time map}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The \vocabk{spinor time map}{fdip-017Z} is antiunitary.
Its square fixes the site labels and acts on their values by
##{S_t^2=\bigl((\Gamma\varepsilon_t)c^{-1}
   (\Gamma\varepsilon_t)c^{-1}\bigr)\otimes c_F^{-2}.}
If #{n=2m} and the chosen grading is
#{C=\ui^m\psI}, with #{\psI=\varepsilon_1\cdots\varepsilon_n}
in the specified direction order, then
##{S_t^2=(-1)^{m+1}c^{-2}\otimes c_F^{-2}.}
The site reflection fixes only the time slice at zero for odd
#{N_t}, and fixes the slices at zero and #{N_t/2} for even
#{N_t}.}

\proof{
\p{On pure tensors the rule #{v\otimes w\mapsto
c^{-1}v\otimes c_F^{-1}w} conjugates a scalar moved across
either factor. It respects the tensor relations, giving one
conjugate-linear map on values. Its inverse uses #{c,c_F} in
the same way. The inner product on two pure tensors is the
product of the two factor inner products. The new product
is its conjugate, and summing over pairs of terms proves the
identity for arbitrary values. Applying it at each site gives
an antiunitary on the full field. Site pullback preserves the
sum by reindexing, and #{\Gamma\varepsilon_t} is unitary as
a product of unitaries. This proves antiunitarity of #{S_t}.}

\p{Site pullback commutes with every constant value action and
squares to the identity. Composing the two remaining actions
on a pure tensor gives the displayed general square. For the
even volume, #{c^{-1}} fixes each direction and conjugates
#{\ui^m} to #{(-1)^m\ui^m}. Consequently
#{c^{-1}(C\varepsilon_t)c=(-1)^m C\varepsilon_t}.
The grading anticommutes with #{\varepsilon_t}, so
#{(C\varepsilon_t)^2=-\bbI_S}. Substitution gives the
stated square without any assumption on the two input squares.}

\p{A time residue #{u} is fixed precisely when #{2u=0}
modulo #{N_t}. For odd period two is invertible, and for
even period #{2M} the solutions are #{0,M}. In the setting
#{M\ge2} of the [closed positive half](fdip-007M), these are
its two boundary slices.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{TensorProduct.congr_tmul} gives the
pure-tensor action of two semilinear equivalences with the
same scalar homomorphism. Here they both conjugate complex
scalars. The Hermitian isometry uses the tensor inner product.}
